## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives (`string`, `number`), change means value equality. For objects and functions, React uses **reference equality** (`===`) — a new object with identical contents is still a different reference.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object reference each time
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new function reference each time
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a brand-new `{}` and `() => {}`, so `===` always returns `false`, and `Child` always re-renders even if nothing visually changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — memoizes a *component*. Skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, config }) => { ... });
```

Use when: a component is pure (output depends only on props) and re-renders are expensive.  
Don't use for: cheap renders — the comparison itself has a cost.

---

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a *value*.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark' }), []);
```

Use when: computing an expensive value, or stabilizing an object/array reference passed as a prop to a memoized child.  
Don't use for: simple literals or values that change every render anyway.

---

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a *function*. Equivalent to `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

Use when: passing a callback to a memoized child component, or as a dependency of another hook.  
Don't use for: functions that aren't passed down or used as dependencies.

**The trio only helps together** — `React.memo` on `Child` is useless if the parent passes a new function reference every render.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every** consumer whenever the context value changes reference — even if the consuming component doesn't use the changed part.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render, all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fixes:

- **Memoize the value** with `useMemo`
- **Split contexts** — separate `UserContext` from `UserDispatchContext` so read-only components don't re-render on dispatch changes
- **Split providers** — stable dispatch context wraps a volatile state context; dispatch consumers never re-render on state changes

```jsx
const UserContext = createContext(null);
const UserDispatchContext = createContext(null);

function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>  {/* stable */}
      <UserContext.Provider value={user}>           {/* changes with user */}
        {children}
      </UserContext.Provider>
    </UserDispatchContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `TaskList` re-renders on every keystroke in the search input, even though tasks didn't change.

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([...]);

  const filtered = tasks.filter(t =>          // new array every render
    t.title.includes(query)
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <TaskList
        tasks={filtered}
        onDelete={(id) => setTasks(t => t.filter(x => x.id !== id))}  // new fn every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function TaskList({ tasks, onDelete }) {  // no memo — always re-renders
  return tasks.map(t => <Task key={t.id} task={t} onDelete={onDelete} />);
}
```

**After** — `TaskList` only re-renders when `filtered` or `onDelete` actually changes.

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([...]);

  const filtered = useMemo(                   // stable reference unless tasks/query change
    () => tasks.filter(t => t.title.includes(query)),
    [tasks, query]
  );

  const handleDelete = useCallback(           // stable reference unless tasks change
    (id) => setTasks(t => t.filter(x => x.id !== id)),
    []                                        // setTasks is stable — no dep needed
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <TaskList tasks={filtered} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

const TaskList = React.memo(function TaskList({ tasks, onDelete }) {
  return tasks.map(t => <Task key={t.id} task={t} onDelete={onDelete} />);
});
```

Now typing in the search input only re-renders `Dashboard` (to update `query`) and `TaskList` if `filtered` actually changes — not on every keystroke if the filtered result is the same.

---

**Rule of thumb:** profile before optimizing. `React.memo` + `useMemo` + `useCallback` all add overhead — only apply them where profiling (React DevTools Profiler) confirms a real problem.
